# **English Contemplation**

*Neti-neti* is not a rejection of life. It is the disciplined refusal to mistake anything perceived for the perceiving Self.

The body is known. Sensations are known. Thoughts, emotions, judgments and even the sense “I am the doer” are known. Whatever is known appears within awareness and therefore cannot be the ultimate knower.

This inquiry does not end in unconscious emptiness. Even the absence of thought is revealed. The Self is not a blank object discovered after everything else has been removed; it is the self-luminous presence by which presence and absence are known.

Knowledge removes the error “I am this limited body-mind.” Yet a final danger remains: the ego may appropriate knowledge itself.

“I conquered desire.”
“I have no ego.”
“I am established as the witness.”

The vocabulary has become spiritual, but the claimant may remain untouched.

Therefore, non-doership is not an excuse for negligence. At the empirical level, ethical responsibility, discipline and purification remain indispensable. At the highest level, action belongs to nature while consciousness remains unchanged.

True non-doership does not weaken action. It removes vanity, anxiety and possessiveness from action.

The final surrender is not the destruction of effort; it is the relinquishment of ownership over effort and result.

Desire may be conquered, but if a proud conqueror remains, defeat remains possible. The deepest victory occurs when the achievement remains useful, yet no separate victor builds a throne upon it.

The Self does not become immortal. It was never born. Knowledge does not manufacture eternity; it removes the mistaken identification with what appears and disappears.

Thus *neti-neti* reaches its fulfilment when even the spiritual claimant is seen as another passing appearance:

Not the body.
Not the mind.
Not the doer.
Not even the proud knower.

What remains is not a personal achievement—but the ever-present light in which the seeker, the path and the victory had appeared.

